<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Han Zhao</h1><p class="st-meta">304 CE – 329 CE · capital Pingyang, Chang'an</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first of the Sixteen Kingdoms, founded by the Xiongnu noble Liu Yuan, whose armies sacked Luoyang in 311 and destroyed the Western Jin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a>, a descendant of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> shanyus educated in the Chinese classics, rebelled during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-eight-princes">War of the Eight Princes</a>. In 304 he proclaimed himself King of Han, claiming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty's</a> legacy through ancestral marriages between Han princesses and shanyus. He became emperor in 308 and in 309 moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pingyang-shanxi">Pingyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state ruled Chinese subjects through a Chinese-style bureaucracy and the tribes through a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> hierarchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-cong">Liu Cong's</a> generals, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yao">Liu Yao</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 311 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 316, taking two Jin emperors prisoner. After a coup in 318, Liu Yao moved the capital to Chang'an and renamed the state Zhao.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a>, who had broken away to found <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-zhao">Later Zhao</a>, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yao">Liu Yao</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 328. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> fell in 329 and the dynasty was wiped out. Han Zhao began three centuries in which northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was ruled by dynasties of steppe origin.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/han-zhao.jpg" alt="Han Zhao" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_317_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>304 CE – 329 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pingyang, Chang'an</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liu Yuan, Liu Cong, Liu Yao</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion and Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese, Xiongnu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Former Zhao, Han (Sixteen Kingdoms)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=316&amp;focus=han-zhao" aria-label="Han Zhao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7490 875 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Han Zhao lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7810 1131L7855 1179M7810 1131L7879 1125"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7879 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7810 1131h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7810 1131h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=316&amp;focus=han-zhao">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

